    "file": "references/a11y.md",
    "title": "Access",
    "body": "# Access\n\nAccessibility is a design constraint, like the grid. The numeric floors and the checklist live in `critique.md`. This file is how to design so those floors are natural instead of patched on at the end.\n\n## Order\n\nThe order of the HTML is the order a person reads and the order a keyboard moves. Do not move things with CSS into a different sequence. If the action is visually last in a dialog, it is last in the DOM too, except where `components.md` puts the safe action first so Enter does not destroy.\n\nOne `h1`. Headings step by one. A heading is not a way to get big type. Big type without a heading is a class (`.display`). A heading without looking like one is still a heading, because the outline of the page is a promise.\n\n## Names\n\nEvery control has a name a person can hear.\n\n- Inputs have a visible label (`components.md`). Placeholder is an example, not the name.\n- Icon buttons name the object and the verb: \"Delete invoice 1842\".\n- Images that carry meaning have alt that says what they show, in the product's voice, not \"image\" and not a filename. Decorative images have empty alt.\n- Links say where they go. \"Read the issue\" is a name. \"Here\" is not.\n- A dialog has a title that is its accessible name.\n\nDo not hide the only name in a tooltip.\n\n## Not color alone\n\nState is a word, a weight, or a mark, plus color. Required is the word Required. Error is a sentence. Current page is weight plus a mark. A red border with no sentence fails people who cannot see the red, and it also fails the quiet design rule that color is a note.\n\nCharts follow `dataviz.md`: labels on the data, not a color legend as the only key.\n\n## Focus and targets\n\nThe ring in `base.css` stays. Design the ring so it looks like it belongs: 2px, offset, `--focus` against the ground. Do not remove it because it clashes with a mock, and do not replace it with a glow.\n\nTargets on a coarse pointer meet the floor in `mobile.md`. A small visual × still has 44px of hit area.\n\n## Motion, contrast, zoom\n\n`prefers-reduced-motion` is already honored in `base.css`. Do not add a second animation path that ignores it. The reduced experience is the same information with no travel: the menu is simply open, the toast is simply there.\n\n`prefers-contrast: more` already strengthens muted text and lines. Do not fade text back down for taste after that.\n\nAt a 200% zoom the page reflows to one column. It does not grow a horizontal scrollbar for the whole layout. That is the same recompose as the 720px breakpoint. Test it.\n\n## Cognitive load\n\nOne idea per section (`narrative.md`). Plain words (`writing.md`). The same kind of action lives in the same place on every screen: primary at the end of the header cluster, destroy in the overflow or at the end of settings.\n\nDo not start a timer the person cannot pause if the content takes reading. Do not flash. Do not autoplay sound.\n\nA long form says how long it is, or it is split into named steps with a way back. Do not wipe answers when someone goes back.\n\n## Live updates\n\nA toast region is polite. A polite region does not interrupt. An error that blocks the work is inline or a banner, not a toast that vanishes. Loading that replaces a whole view announces itself in text if it takes long enough that the person might think the page died (\"Loading orders\").\n\nDo not move focus to a notification. Leave the person where they were, unless you are taking them to the error they must fix, and then focus moves to that field on submit only (`interaction.md`).",
